WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.210
Psychedelic drugs are commonly reported

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.683
to trigger life-altering,
mind-expanding inward journeys.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.640
But since being scheduled
dangerous and illegal,

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.427
any therapeutic benefits have
gone largely unrecognized.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:30.650
Mental health treatment
hasn't changed in generations,

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.850
limited primarily to psychotherapy

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.580
and a broad drug class called SSRIs,

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.793
or selective serotonin
re-uptake inhibitors.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.990
There's been no new drug in psychiatry

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:46.010
essentially since SSRIs back in the 1980s.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.160
These drugs have varying results,

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.530
often with side effects,

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.930
and can be seriously habit-forming.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.730
Once you get on one SSRI,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.750
they've got you for life, pretty much.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.660
But this model could soon be disrupted

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.090
by the acceptance of
psychedelics by the medical,

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.593
psychiatric, and
pharmacological communities.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.410
This is now coming at the forefront

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.770
after spending 60 years in hiding.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.460
And these tools actually have
remarkable healing potential

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.693
and have had for thousands of years.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:18.420
LSD, psilocybin, DMT, MDMA,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.820
and other consciousness-altering compounds

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.310
once labeled dangerous and illegal drugs,

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.640
are coming into the political spotlight.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.300
A recent ballot measure in Oregon

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.590
authorized the legal use of psilocybin

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.660
by licensed providers.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.600
Clinical trials are pressing forward

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.050
in what could forever change the way

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:38.603
we understand and treat
both the mind and the body.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.960
Investors are flocking
to stake their claim

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.350
in this new frontier of magic molecules.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.473
What is really driving us here is the fact

00:01:48.473 --> 00:01:51.070
that the efficacy rates
here cannot be ignored.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:55.930
They're profound, and
money will follow efficacy.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:58.770
But these investors aren't
just hedging their bets.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.180
They're true believers

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.490
intent on flooding psychedelic startups

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:03.830
with the capital they need

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:06.203
to invoke a revolution of the mind.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.640
The word psychedelic is derived

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.560
from the ancient Greek
word psyche, meaning soul,

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.150
and deloun, meaning to reveal.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.820
It translates to mind-manifesting,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.140
and the first real documented
use of psychedelics

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.880
can actually be traced
back to that same era.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.880
From 1600 BC to 396 AD,

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:40.770
in the Greek culture, the
foundations of Western culture,

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:44.000
they had the longest-running
mystery ceremonies ever.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.810
And they were involved
with a psychedelic drug,

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:48.290
a potion called kykeon,

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:51.130
which we now through modern
scholarship have identified

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:52.703
as being similar to LSD.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.470
Rick Doblin is the founder of

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.040
the Multidisciplinary Association
for Psychedelic Studies,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.140
or MAPS.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.940
Our top priority project is
MDMA-assisted psychotherapy

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.170
for post-traumatic stress disorder,

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.730
and we're in phase III studies right now.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.570
So, Rick, how long have
you been involved in this?

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:12.143
I started MAPS in '86.

00:03:13.203 --> 00:03:14.840
In the upcoming scene,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.410
we will watch a young man

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.560
who is having a difficult experience

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.280
while under the influence
of a psychedelic drug.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.840
I really got involved

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.990
in trying to devote my
life to psychedelics

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:28.090
and becoming a psychedelic
therapist in 1972.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:30.700
So, I imagine things have
changed quite a bit since then?

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.550
Oh, my God, everything has changed.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:38.120
America's public enemy
number one is drug abuse.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.500
When I began, it was the
depth of the backlash.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.980
In 1970, the Controlled Substances Act

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.020
was passed in the United States.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.500
Psychedelics were criminalized.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.470
The research was squashed.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:51.220
Extensive experimentation

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:53.683
has failed to establish
a medical use for LSD.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:55.963
After Nixon,

00:03:55.963 --> 00:03:59.060
mainstream acceptance of
psychedelics was impossible,

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:01.090
and these compounds went underground

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.270
where it stayed for over a half century,

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.883
associated with
counterculture and degeneracy.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.200
Taking LSD is much the same

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.320
as playing Russian roulette.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.370
What are you doing out here?

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.330
I'm floating, I'm floating
up to the stars, Joe!

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:16.190
You can't float out here,
we're three stories up!

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:17.480
No, I'm one with the universe!

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.320
I'm God and Jesus, Joe!

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.830
But guys like Rick Doblin

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:23.760
continued their research on the fringes.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.390
And so, what's changed now

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:28.490
is the public attitudes have changed.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:32.740
The regulatory agencies
are open to research.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.730
We just completed a $30
million capstone campaign

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.760
for our MDMA PTSD phase III studies

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.660
and for commercialization.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.920
We're in a major psychedelic
Renaissance right now.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.330
And now we have these new
tools for neuroscience

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.633
to really understand what
these drugs do in the brain.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.712
But that is not an easy
question to answer.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:00.630
There are a lot of drugs out there

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:02.090
that we don't know how they work.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:04.840
You only have to show that a drug is safe

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:06.240
and that it's effective.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.220
You don't have to know how a
drug works to get it approved.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.270
I mean, asking how psychedelics work

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.530
seems almost as challenging as asking,

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:15.921
what is the meaning of life?

00:05:15.921 --> 00:05:19.320
I think if we have the answer to one,

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:20.920
we'll have the answer to the other.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.530
Dr. Charles Nichols is
professor of pharmacology

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:26.570
at Louisiana State University
Health Sciences Center.

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:29.920
I primarily study
serotonin neuropharmacology

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:31.750
with a focus on psychedelic drugs.

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:33.460
So, tell me a little
bit about what we know

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:37.480
is happening to the brain
on, say, LSD or psilocybin.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:41.490
What the psychedelics do is they target

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:46.490
a selective subset of serotonin receptors.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:49.260
The one that produces
the psychedelic effects

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.220
is called the serotonin 2A receptor.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:54.280
A lot of positive effects are associated

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:56.480
with activation of that receptor.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.410
Okay, so how about a less
scientific explanation?

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:02.700
Unscientifically, what
psychedelics do in the brain,

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.930
you're having several brain areas

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.580
that are talking to one another

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:08.580
that don't normally talk to one another,

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.710
and you've established new
connections, new pathways.

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:16.560
There's one theory that posits that when

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.150
that trip starts to wear off,

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:21.870
the network will reset more into

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.020
a normal connectivity state

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.310
than, say, if somebody was depressed.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:29.580
So, what kind of tests
in the lab do you perform

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:30.990
to devise these theories?

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:34.360
We've developed two rat-based models

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.950
where we can essentially
create a depressed rat,

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:41.290
give it a single dose of psilocybin,

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:44.310
and the rats that were given psilocybin

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:46.460
are now acting like normal rats.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:47.470
They're not depressed.

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:50.660
I'm curious, how do you
know a rat is depressed?

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:53.610
Oh, it just kind of
hangs out in its corner.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:54.963
Doesn't like to do much.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:57.710
But these laboratory observations

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:01.390
don't speculate on what
happens during the actual trip.

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:04.163
And this is where the
therapy really comes in.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.790
It's not a question of whether
the science permits it,

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.550
but it's how we use these tools.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:14.810
These are powerful tools,

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:16.650
but we still haven't quite figured out,

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:20.310
or at least we're still working
on the best way to use them.

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:23.270
Shlomi Raz is CEO and founder of Eleusis.

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:25.250
He, along with other
up and coming startups,

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:28.460
are designing the methodology
to safely and effectively

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:29.960
practice psychedelic medicine.

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:36.470
Much of their inspiration comes

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.220
from ancient shamanic practices,

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:40.810
such as ayahuasca
ceremonies in South America,

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:43.090
which have become increasingly
popular among people

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:44.940
seeking alternative forms of healing

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:47.240
after traditional
treatments have failed them.

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:51.250
So, Shlomi, what's the
most important thing

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:53.600
we can learn from these
shamanistic practices

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.610
as you design modern
therapeutic applications?

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:59.890
That this clinical
rollout be done in a way

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:03.170
that treats the therapeutic potential

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:04.990
and power of these drugs with respect.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:07.420
And I think that would be the
biggest mistake is that if,

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:09.480
in a rush to commercialization,

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:13.350
that there is a loss of respect
associated with development.

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:15.430
Maybe you can walk me through your vision

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:17.150
of a psychedelic therapy session.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:20.550
I think number one, this is
not the job of psychiatrist.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:23.210
Number two, we're not
sure that you even want

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:25.060
a psychotherapist in the room.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:29.220
Instead, the person that we
think should be in the room

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:30.220
is an attendant,

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:33.050
someone where you feel
safe in their presence.

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:35.840
Are they certified, you
know, medical professionals?

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.130
Not necessarily,

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:40.880
but they bring an instant
rapport and an instant comfort.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:42.800
And what they're comfortable with

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.180
is emotionally intense situations.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.300
With this process, the
patients heal themselves,

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:49.950
and the need for antidepressants

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:53.630
or other long-term prescription
drugs could fade away.

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:55.200
But the potential for these compounds

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.210
go far beyond mental health treatment.

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:59.990
Multiple studies have found
that regular minute doses

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:01.980
of LSD and similar compounds

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.360
have enormous anti-inflammatory effects,

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:06.410
potentially offering
alternative treatments

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:09.150
to everything from asthma
to migraine headaches.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.630
You know, we have some
very interesting clues

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:14.240
that there's much more
therapeutic potential

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:15.703
just beneath the surface.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:20.880
All of this is not only revolutionary,

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:22.460
but potentially disruptive

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:25.827
to the estimated $16 billion
antidepressant drug market.

00:09:25.827 --> 00:09:27.330
When we take a look at some

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:28.850
of these compounds and molecules,

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:32.330
there are 70%, 75%, 80% efficacy rates.

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:34.653
So, that is, that's profound,

00:09:34.653 --> 00:09:37.340
and that will disrupt Big Pharma.

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:40.500
Sa'ad Shaw leads a venture
fund called Noetic,

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.860
and he is what you would
call a true believer.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:44.590
I mean, it's definitely very personal.

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:46.440
It's personal, first and foremost,

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.483
because I personally have,
you know, drunk the brew.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:55.170
I had an experience with plant medicine

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.410
and it was profound on many levels.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:00.740
I saw the distinct impact that it had

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:04.100
on folks that were suffering from cancer,

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.730
individuals that were suffering

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:08.840
from very serious
treatment-resistant depression,

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:12.290
major depressive disorders, PTSD, anxiety.

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:14.973
I knew then and there that
there was something here

00:10:14.973 --> 00:10:17.110
that was incredibly profound.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:18.570
Okay, so there's certainly a motivation

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:20.270
to keep people on drugs,

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:22.750
and these compounds often do the opposite,

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:26.290
which essentially means
there's less money to be made.

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:29.540
How is Big Pharma not trying
to squash this movement?

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:33.803
If you're finding a market
and a product is working,

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:36.770
and clearly you've got
underlying companies

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:39.470
are gonna profit as a result of it,

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:41.500
this is gonna be of
interest to Big Pharma.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:43.820
I think that they will get
more and more involved,

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:46.030
and I think it's important
for them to get involved

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:48.030
because at the end of the day,

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:52.083
we need this medicine to
go out there to the globe,

00:10:52.083 --> 00:10:54.410
and you need a distribution platform.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:56.920
Big Pharma has that distribution platform.

00:10:56.920 --> 00:10:59.020
They're the ones that can
help get it out there.

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:00.590
But that optimism might be putting

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:03.430
a little too much faith in
a profit-based apparatus

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:05.650
that has enabled severe drug dependencies,

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:07.770
like the opioid crisis in America.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:09.930
For these compounds to truly scale,

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:11.540
it's not only our minds,

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:14.680
but our entire society
that might need a rewiring.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:18.330
There needs to definitely be a redefining

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:20.040
of profit, of capitalism.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:24.030
Profit needs to first be
measured in human capital, right?

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:25.870
Healing those who have suffered,

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:27.860
if we do that right,

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:30.330
our investment in the
companies will succeed.

